<section class="shell model-brief retail-brief" aria-labelledby="retail-model">
  <h2 id="retail-model">The allocation problem</h2>
  <dl class="model-contract">
    <div><dt>The decision</dt><dd>At the start of a week, choose a whole number of units for each of 30 products. The product list is selected from the training period and stays fixed, including products with no later recorded activity.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>The objective</dt><dd>Minimize expected shortfall and leftover units across past weekly scenarios. Shortfall priority is a preference: at 80%, a shortfall unit receives four times the weight of a leftover unit.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>The information</dt><dd>Use only complete earlier weeks. Past positive invoice quantities estimate the range of activity to plan for; the following week’s recorded activity is used to evaluate the allocation.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>The constraints</dt><dd>Total allocated units cannot exceed the shared budget. Quantities are nonnegative integers. An optional product minimum reserves units within that same budget; an impossible minimum is reported as infeasible.</dd></div>
  </dl>
  <p class="model-control-explanation"><strong>The budget and priority are scenario inputs.</strong> A 100% budget equals the rounded total of the preceding eight-week mean quantities. All products consume one unit of this abstract resource per item. This does not measure warehouse space, stock on hand or money available.</p>
  <details class="data-details model-formulation">
    <summary>Variables, objective and solution method</summary>
    <p>For product <var>i</var>, choose integer allocation <var>q</var><sub>i</sub> at least its optional minimum <var>m</var><sub>i</sub>. Scenario <var>s</var> supplies past weekly activity <var>d</var><sub>is</sub>. Each of the selected history window’s weeks has equal probability. The priority <var>λ</var> is strictly between zero and one.</p>
    <ul class="model-equations">
      <li>∑ <var>q</var><sub>i</sub> ≤ <var>B</var></li>
      <li><var>q</var><sub>i</sub> ≥ <var>m</var><sub>i</sub> ≥ 0; whole units</li>
      <li>Shortfall: max(<var>d</var><sub>is</sub> − <var>q</var><sub>i</sub>, 0)</li>
      <li>Leftover: max(<var>q</var><sub>i</sub> − <var>d</var><sub>is</sub>, 0)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Minimize the scenario average of ∑[<var>λ</var> × shortfall + (1 − <var>λ</var>) × leftover]. This score is measured in weighted units. There are no selling prices, costs or margins in the objective.</p>
    <p>The exact marginal-allocation algorithm assigns the next unit where it most reduces that score. It stops when the budget is exhausted or no positive reduction remains. Ties use the fewest total units, then product-code order. The additive objective uses each product’s marginal distribution; it does not model an additional benefit from correlations between products.</p>
    <p>The eight-, thirteen- and twenty-six-week history windows are compared on validation data; one window is then fixed for the test period. Earlier completed test weeks may update the rolling history, but test results cannot change that choice.</p>
  </details>
  <div class="strategy-brief">
    <h3>Compare against simple allocation rules</h3>
    <dl class="strategy-list">
      <div><dt>Eight-week mean</dt><dd>Target the recent average for each product. If the budget is tight, divide it in proportion to those targets after reserving any minimum.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Same week last year</dt><dd>Use quantities recorded 52 weeks earlier, with the same budget-allocation rule.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Equal allocation</dt><dd>Spread the available target total evenly across products, respecting any minimum.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Perfect-information bound</dt><dd>Allocate knowing the following week’s recorded activity in advance. This is an unattainable comparison at decision time.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
  <p class="model-scope"><strong>Scope:</strong> a one-week allocation exercise using an unnamed UK retailer’s 2009–2011 records. Positive invoices can later be cancelled and may reflect unobserved availability. Shortfall is against recorded activity, not measured lost sales. There is no inventory carry-over, replenishment lead time or claim of business savings.</p>
</section>
